Smart Attendance Management 


Project Documentation 


1.	Working Solution / Prototype 
A working web-based Smart Attendance Management System designed to digitize the college attendance process. 
•	Faculty can mark daily attendance. 
•	Faculty can request corrections for previous attendance. 
•	HOD/Admin can approve or reject correction requests. 
•	The system calculates attendance percentage. 
•	It identifies students with attendance below the configured threshold. 
•	Student attendance can be searched and viewed. 
•	Audit logs and campus notices are supported. 
•	The prototype can run locally using Node.js on port 3000. 
2.	Source Code / Repository 
The project contains the following main files: 
•	student.html — Frontend UI, CSS and JavaScript logic. 
•	server.js — Node.js backend and REST API. 
•	data.json — Persistent JSON storage for attendance, requests, audit information and notices. 
Architecture 
HTML + CSS + JavaScript → REST API / Fetch → Node.js → data.json 
3.	Brief Explanation of Approach, Assumptions, Architecture and Trade-offs 
Approach 
The system digitizes the college attendance process. Faculty marks attendance through the frontend, while the Node.js backend handles API requests and stores the application state. 
Assumptions 
•	The system is designed for a college environment. 
•	Users have different roles such as Faculty, HOD and Admin. 
•	A configurable attendance threshold is used to identify low-attendance students. 
•	The current prototype uses browser localStorage for application state/data storage rather than a traditional database. 
Architecture 
•	Frontend: HTML, CSS and JavaScript. 
•	Backend: Node.js using the HTTP module. 
•	API: REST-style endpoints using the Fetch API. 
•	Storage: Browser localStorage in the current prototype. 
Trade-offs 
•	localStorage: Simple and easy to use for a prototype, but it is not suitable for centralized, multi-user production storage. 
•	Node.js built-in HTTP: Keeps the project lightweight because there are no external backend dependencies, but a production application could use a framework and database. 
•	Role-based workflow: Adds better control over corrections and administration but makes the application logic more complex. 
